<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">International Workingmen's Association</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1864">1864</a>–<a href="/years/1876">1876</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as First International, International Working Men's Association</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The International Workingmen's Association, commonly known as the First International, was a political international that aimed to unite left-wing groups and trade unions of the working class. It was founded at a meeting of some 2,000 workers in St Martin's Hall, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, on 28 September 1864, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a> drafted its Inaugural Address and Provisional Rules. Conflict between Marx's supporters and the anarchists around Mikhail Bakunin led to a split at the Hague Congress of 1872, which expelled Bakunin and moved the General Council to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. The International was formally disbanded in 1876.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/international-workingmens-association.jpg" alt="International Workingmen's Association" width="320" height="271" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:International-founding-1864.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
